[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch1
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the VLAN 10 trunk towards the distribution switch. PCs on VLAN 10 (172.16.10.0/24) can talk to each other locally but cannot ARP the gateway, while VLAN 20 is unaffected. From pc4/pc5, ARP to 172.16.10.1 fails (“172.16.10.1 dev eth0 FAILED”), yet inter-host pings on VLAN 10 succeed, confirming the access segment on switch1 is fine. Router0’s VLAN interfaces are up (eth0.10 has 172.16.10.1/24), so the L3 gateway is available if VLAN 10 traffic can reach it.

Localization: On switch1, the uplink trunk subinterfaces are up and bridged correctly (eth0.10 master br_vlan10; eth0.20 master br_vlan20; all forwarding). On mswitch1, however, the matching trunk for VLAN 10 is down/blocked: eth0.10 is DOWN and “bridge_slave state disabled” under br_vlan10, while eth0.20 is UP and forwarding. Other VLAN 10 paths on mswitch1 (eth2.10 to switch2 and eth4.10 to router0) are UP, which isolates the break to the mswitch1<->switch1 trunk on VLAN 10.

Root cause: The VLAN 10 subinterface on mswitch1 (eth0.10) is administratively down/disabled, breaking the L2 path for VLAN 10 between switch1 and mswitch1.

Corrective fix: Re-enable VLAN 10 on mswitch1’s uplink to switch1 (e.g., ip link set dev eth0.10 up; ensure it remains enslaved to br_vlan10 and STP not blocking). After bringing eth0.10 up, ARP from 172.16.10.4/5 to 172.16.10.1 should resolve and gateway reachability will be restored. === END ===